# Fleet EOL-normalization baseline (#282 target shape; W3 hub leg, 2026-07-13).
# All text files normalize to LF in the repo and on checkout; PowerShell scripts
# check out CRLF (Windows-native). The generated-file pins below predate this
# baseline and stay as ADDITIVE defense-in-depth (last match wins).
* text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# LF-pin generated floor hash sidecars (#137).
# The floor hash is computed over LF-normalized bytes (scripts/generate_floor.py), so
# git autocrlf re-normalizing these to CRLF on Windows produced a content-identical
# "phantom" dirty-tree entry on every touch (witnessed 2026-06-08 floor pilot). Pinning
# the sidecars — and the template the hash is computed over — to LF stops the phantom.
*.sha256 text eol=lf
templates/child-methodology-floor.md.tmpl text eol=lf

# LF-pin generated PASTE_THIS boot payloads (audit F3, 2026-06-15).
# scripts/assemble_paste.py writes LF (newline="\n"); without this pin, autocrlf checks
# the file out as CRLF, so re-running the assembler (as HANDOFF_PROCESS §13 instructs:
# "regenerate each handoff by re-running the assembler") rewrites it LF and produces a
# content-identical phantom dirty-tree entry. Same fix as the floor sidecars above.
docs/handoffs/**/PASTE_THIS.md text eol=lf

# LF-pin the deploy version-record registry (ADR-91/92; deploy-lf-normalize).
# The deploy tool writes ai-council's record on a hub branch via git plumbing
# (hash-object). The writer now emits LF bytes, but pin the file too so any
# NON-plumbing touch (a hand edit on a Windows checkout) normalizes to LF and
# never re-introduces the CRLF whole-file flip that blocked the first record
# merge. Same phantom-churn fix as the generated files above; defense-in-depth.
ecosystem/deployed-versions.yaml text eol=lf

# [#590] — the audits index is REGENERATED, never merge-resolved.
# `docs/audits/README.md` is a generated index over a directory every parallel lane writes
# into. Measured over the last 20 first-parent merges (hub diagnostic 2026-08-26 s5.3): 7
# needed manual resolution and this ONE file appears in 6 of them — 86% of all manual merge
# resolution in this repo, caused by a file no human authors. Two lanes each add an audit,
# each regenerates, and the inserted rows plus the `**N audit documents.**` count collide.
#
# `merge=ours` makes git keep the receiving side and stop diffing it. The result is
# deliberately STALE — the incoming lane's audit is missing from it — which is why this
# attribute is only ONE HALF of the mechanism. The other half is that the index is
# regenerated at INTEGRATION and a ship-gate leg refuses a stale committed one
# (`generated_artifact_freshness`, artifact `audits-index`). Retention is untouched: ADR-100
# keep-all-accepted decides what the tree HOLDS; this decides how its INDEX is produced.
#
# ARMING IS LOCAL AND NOT DISTRIBUTED BY THIS FILE. `ours` is not a built-in driver — git
# resolves it via `merge.ours.driver`, and WITHOUT that config the attribute silently does
# NOTHING and the merge conflicts exactly as before (verified empirically, 2026-08-26).
# `scripts/arm_hooks.py` sets it at SessionStart alongside the git hooks, and
# `audit.py::check_hooks_armed` asserts it, so configured-but-unarmed fails loud rather than
# looking armed. To arm by hand: `git config --local merge.ours.driver true`.
docs/audits/README.md merge=ours
